  "question": {
    "text": "The integral of $\\frac{x^2-x}{x^3-x^2+x-1}$ w.r.t. $x$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "1PGgI",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{2} \\log \\left(x^2+1+c\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "frf4K",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{2} \\log \\left|x^2-1\\right|+c$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Utdm2",
      "text": "\n$\\log \\left(x^2+1+c\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ETWDE",
      "text": "\n$\\log \\left|x^2-1\\right|+c$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $I=\\int \\frac{x^2-x}{x^3-x^2+x-1} d x$\n$$\n=\\int \\frac{x(x-1)}{x^2(x-1)+(x-1)} d x=\\int \\frac{x d x}{x^2+1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left.=\\frac{1}{2} \\int \\frac{2 x d x}{\\left(x^2+1\\right.}\\right)\n$$\nLet $x^2+1=t \\Rightarrow 2 x d x=d t$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\therefore I=\\frac{1}{2} \\int \\frac{d t}{t}=\\frac{1}{2} \\log t+c \\\\\n& =\\frac{1}{2} \\log \\left(x^2+1\\right)+c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere ' $c$ ' is the constant of integration.",
    "image": null
  },
